We try

48
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:22,000
In this podcast. I'm kicking off what I hope will be several different interviews over the course of 2012 to recognize the 100th anniversary of the Eagle Scout Award.

49
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:28,000
I've been working at contacting notable Eagle Scouts trying to line them up for interviews and things like that,

50
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:39,000
And I was really pleased- and, I got to tell you, slightly amazed- that our Chief Scout Executive, Bob Mazzucca, who is also an Eagle Scout,

51
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,000
replied to my request for an interview by sitting down and recording an interview with me.

52
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:53,000
I wasn't quite so interested in his work as our Chief Scout Executive as his history as a scout and as an Eagle Scout.

53
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
So I think- and Bob certainly had some fond memories to relate to us of that time in his life,

54
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:08,000
And he's got some advice for us and a little bit of news about what's coming in the, what's happening in the coming years as far as the BSA is involved.

55
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
So I'm going to move right into the interview

56
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:17,000
And after that there's a piece about the symbol of the Eagle and a little bit of the history of the Eagle Scout.

57
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
So hey, that's plenty for one podcast, wouldn't you say?

58
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:21,000
That's what I would say.

59
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
So let's get started, shall we?

60
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:48,000
48 years ago, my guest was in San Juan Batista, California.

61
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:53,000
He was 16 years old and he was about to become Troop 28's newest Eagle Scout.

62
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Now he's a pretty average scout, a pretty average, above average scout.

63
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:01,000
He spent a couple of summers on staff at Pico Blanco Scout Reservation.

64
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:04,000
He was inducted into the Esalen Lodge Order of the Arrow-

65
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
Very similar story to a lot of guys in scouting-

66
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Went on to college and soon thereafter he started working for the Boy Scouts of America.

67
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:16,000
He continued as a scouting professional for the next 36 years

68
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:21,000
until he was selected to be our Chief Scout Executive in 2007..

69
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:27,000
And it is my great pleasure to welcome our Chief Scout Executive, Robert Mazuka, to the Scout Master Podcast.

70
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Hello Bob,

71
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,000
Hello Clark, how are you?

72
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
I am just doing great

73
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:37,000
And I invited you to come and talk with us today to recognize the 100th anniversary of the Eagle Scout Award.

74
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,000
I'm working to interview a number of notable Eagle Scouts

75
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:47,000
And what I'm really interested in is the story of that kid back there in San Juan Batista in Troop 28..

76
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
How did you get started with Troop 28??

77
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
I was actually. if you've got a minute, it's kind of an interesting story.

78
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:57,000
I'm son of an Italian immigrant, one of eight kids.

79
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:04,000
My dad was trying to figure out how to raise a family and we moved around a little bit. in my first few years of life.

80
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:09,000
We ended up in San Juan Batista when I was eight years old

81
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
And we didn't know anything about Cub Scouting.

82
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
I was never a Cub Scout but I got involved in the school and stuff.

83
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:24,000
And then my neighbor Abraham Vega came over one day and he was wearing this funny uniform

84
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:27,000
And he asked me if I wanted to go with him to a troop meeting.

85
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,000
I was 11 at the time, I guess,

86
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,000
And I went and drank the Kool-Aid and never went back.

87
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:35,000
It was really exciting,

88
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:38,000
One of those fun kind of things. there's a really cool group of guys,

89
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:42,000
a very eclectic group all over the map in terms of the intro,

90
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:45,000
And the Scoutmaster was really a neat guy, Roy Pedersen.

91
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:49,000
And so I got started and got involved. a little while later

92
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:52,000
I became a Den Chief and that was my first exposure to Cub Scouting.

93
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:54,000
I didn't know what a Cub Scout was

94
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:58,000
And then I became the patrol leader of the Cobra Patrol.

95
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,000
The typical story was a great adventure

96
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:04,000
And it sort of kind of formed me.

97
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:07,000
It was an interesting. I didn't know what was happening at the time,

98
00:08:07,000 --> 00:08:10,000
but I was sort of beginning to blossom and do things

99
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
that I probably wouldn't have done had it not been for Scouting.

100
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
I played football as well and baseball in high school.

101
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:23,000
But my interest in getting involved really kind of stemmed from that invitation

102
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:25,000
by a neighbor to go join a Scout troop.

103
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:29,000
You guys did what all other Scouts in the world do:

104
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:33,000
You camped, you hiked, you hung out together, had your patrol improved.

105
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:35,000
It was kind of an ideal time, though

106
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:37,000
It was kind of a different time.

107
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:42,000
Please don't let anybody get the idea you can do this today,

108
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:46,000
But we would actually go out camping as a patrol by ourselves.

109
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:50,000
We'd have a camp out and go out to a neighbor's ranch or farm

110
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:54,000
and camp as a patrol and that sort of thing.

111
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
It was a fairly ideal.

112
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
It was a very small town of about a thousand people

113
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
and the house was never locked,

114
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:06,000
The keys were always in the car, that kind of era.

115
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:13,000
So we were a lot more opportunity to just be boys

116
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
and inventive and creative in ways that don't exist today,

117
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:20,000
and that's a challenge for Scouting that we need to understand

118
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:23,000
as well as we talk about the current time.

119
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
But for me it was just a great adventure.

120
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Can you point to any individual person

121
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:32,000
who really made a difference in your life at the time?

122
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:34,000
Sure, Ken, I can. three

123
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Besides my father. my father was my hero,

124
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,000
But the scout master Roy Pederson was just a great man.

125
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:46,000
He was a park ranger at the state park there in San Juan.

126
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:48,000
The mission and all of that historic grounds was a state park

127
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
and he was a park ranger.

128
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:51,000
I had no kids of his own.

129
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
He and his wife were just delightful people

130
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
and he was a wonderful man.

131
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Milt Harrell was kind of the godfather of Scouting for decades.

132
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:05,000
As a matter of fact I suspect that he had something to do with this.

133
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:09,000
When I joined Scouting we didn't have anything

134
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:12,000
and I didn't know where I was going to ever get a uniform

135
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:14,000
or a cook kit or anything like that.

136
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,000
One day they just kind of showed up

137
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:18,000
and I didn't know where they came from.

138
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:20,000
but I suspect that old Uncle Milt did that.

139
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:24,000
He was just kind of the godfather of the glue.

140
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
that sort of held Scouting together in our town.

141
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:29,000
There was only one troop and one pack in this little town

142
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:34,000
And then much further along. as I love telling Scout Masters

143
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:38,000
this story because, like it or not, good or bad,

144
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
kids are listening to you,

145
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
They really are listening to what you're saying.

146
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
I was on the camp staff at Camp Picoblanco

147
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
and I had just graduated.

148
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
It was my third year on the staff

149
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:52,000
From high school, when I was spending the summer on the staff

150
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:55,000
and I was going to go to college that fall,

151
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:57,000
I remember toward the end of the season,

152
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Bill Litterdale was the camp director.

153
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:01,000
I just worshiped this guy.

154
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
He was a great baritone, great singer.

155
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
He had a bit of boxer in college

156
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:09,000
and then the Navy in his nose was spread all across his face.

157
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
That's a great guy.

158
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
But one day toward the end of the season

159
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
he sort of took me aside and we sat on a log

160
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
and he just started talking to me

161
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
about what I was going to do with myself.

162
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
I had planned all my life to be a teacher.

163
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
I wanted to be a school teacher.

164
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
I was going to college to be educated

165
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
so I could be a school teacher.

166
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
He suggested that I might want to think about professional scouting.

167
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
He thought that he had seen something in me

168
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
that would make me a reasonably effective professional scouter.

169
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
And here's a guy that I just worshiped

170
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
telling me that he thought I was good enough

171
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
to do what he does for a living.

172
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
I was just blown away

173
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
And so that was always in the back of my mind.

174
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:51,000
And as I got through school and graduated,

175
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
I applied to be to scouting

176
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
and there was no way. frankly, I did teach school for one year

177
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
simply because I needed a job.

178
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:02,000
I had a little Catholic school in San Luis Obispo.

179
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
I was actually working in the gas station

180
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
and I was back in the days when you washed windows and stuff

181
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:09,000
and I was washing the windows of this lady

182
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
who was getting gas and she gave me her credit card

183
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
and it was Mission Nativity Schools

184
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
and it happened to be Sister Clarice

185
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:18,000
and I was graduating that spring

186
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,000
and I said, man, I need a job,

187
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:24,000
I'm a history major. and she says: I need a history teacher.

188
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:27,000
My first year out of college I taught school

189
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:31,000
but I got a call about midway through that contract here

190
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
that there was an opening in Modesto

191
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
and scouting and I went over and interviewed

192
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
and came back, fulfilled my contract with the school

193
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:41,000
and then started that July in Modesto in 1971

194
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
and I never looked back.

195
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
It's been a great career,

196
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
But it was that man, that man I worshiped, that man I respected.

197
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
Had he been a plumber

198
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
and told me that he thought I could be a good plumber,

199
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
I probably would be a plumber today.

200
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
That's how much I thought of Bill Hitterdale.

201
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:56,000
He's a great guy,

202
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:59,000
Obviously. that was just an amazing turning point

203
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:02,000
and you've carried a lot of that forward.

204
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:06,000
What do you remember about becoming an Eagle Scout?

205
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
that may have been a little bit different at the time

206
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:09,000
than it is now.

207
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
Well, it was different. frankly,

208
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
I racked my brains for years

209
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:16,000
because I read and I see all of these wonderful service projects

210
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
that are happening out there.

211
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
I couldn't remember my service project.

212
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
Maybe it's in my record

213
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:25,000
and they reminded me that there wasn't a service project back then

214
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
and I became an Eagle Scout.

215
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
So I think, oh God, thank you,

216
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
because I thought I would do something memorable

217
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
if I had done something.

218
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
But the thing that I-

219
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:38,000
and I've been asked this question before-

220
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:42,000
what about the process was most beneficial to you

221
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
or most impactful on you?

222
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:47,000
And for me it was learning to be a leader.

223
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:50,000
I just did 21 merit badges.

224
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:53,000
I did the required. I wasn't one to get all the merit badges

225
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
or anything like that.

226
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:59,000
I was fascinated with this emerging part of me

227
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:02,000
that could be a leader

228
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:05,000
And I learned so much from that process

229
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
about servant leadership

230
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
and community service and all of that,

231
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:12,000
and I learned that it felt pretty good to be a good leader,

232
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
and I learned that in scouting,

233
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:19,000
and so I would say that that was the most beneficial to me

234
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
was learning the leadership skills

235
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
that are still with you today, no matter what you do.

236
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
If you had the opportunity to talk to that young guy

237
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
then- that 16-year-old-

238
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:31,000
you had to have had a time

239
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
where you were a little discouraged about doing this,

240
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
or how to step back, or two.

241
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:36,000
What was your talent?

242
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
Well, first of all, I would say

243
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
perseverance is absolutely the key

244
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
and it's imperative.

245
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
And the reason I say it's imperative

246
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
because this is an everyday occurrence for me. today

247
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
I wear a lapel pin on my coat wherever I go,

248
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
because it's a great conversation starter,

249
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
and it goes kind of something like this:

250
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
You go to take your coat off on the plane

251
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
and your seat mate sees the lapel pin

252
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
and he says, oh, that's a scout pin, right,

253
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
And you say, yeah, it is.

254
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
It begins a process of going down memory lane

255
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
with his scouting experience.

256
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
It's just like an itch just waiting to be scratched,

257
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
But invariably what happens-

258
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
and I meet these people every day

259
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
who will tell me that their biggest regret

260
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
in their entire life-

261
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
and they've carried it with them for their whole life-

262
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
is that they didn't close the deal,

263
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
They didn't become an Eagle Scout,

264
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,000
Especially if they made it to star or life,

265
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
Especially life.

266
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:28,000
And I would say to a young man

267
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
who was struggling with kind of closing the deal

268
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
that he will regret it for the rest of his life,

269
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
and I know this for a fact.

270
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
This isn't just some philosophical big guy,

271
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
old guy, talking to you here,

272
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
Number one and number two.

273
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
the other reason why it's really really important

274
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,000
is the world we live in today needs Eagle Scouts.

275
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:49,000
We need the kind of character,

276
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
leadership with integrity,

277
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
sense of service

278
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
and all of those kinds of things

279
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
that goes along with becoming and being

280
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
an Eagle Scout for the rest of your life.

281
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:02,000
So, for a very personal, selfish reason,

282
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
you'll regret it if you don't-

283
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
and for a much more global reason,

284
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
we need you to be an Eagle Scout.

285
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
We're talking to Scoutmasters right now.

286
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
We're talking to Scoutmasters,

287
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
assistant Scoutmasters, people on committees

288
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
and things like that.

289
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
Is it important for us to be driving these guys

290
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
towards becoming Eagle Scouts?

291
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
Well, I think we have to be careful about driving them

292
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
to become an Eagle Scout,

293
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
because I'll give you a personal example

294
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
when I first started in my career:

295
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
the relationship between my first boss

296
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
and his son destroyed

297
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
for life.

298
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
He went to his grave. he strayed from his son

299
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
over the dad's passion

300
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
for him to become an Eagle Scout-

301
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
for the dad's reasons and not the boys.

302
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
There's lots of things that accrue

303
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
to a young person's involvement in Scouting.

304
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
By the way, my older son's not an Eagle Scout

305
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
but he got a tremendous amount out of Scouting

306
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
And I think that,

307
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
for example, as a matter of fact,

308
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
I would say to anybody who thinks

309
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
we can measure our success by Eagle Scouts:

310
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
if that were the case, we'd be dismal failures.

311
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
Five percent of the kids become Eagle Scouts.

312
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
There's a number of reasons why,

313
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
you know,

314
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
either the leadership isn't strong

315
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
or there's a number of reasons

316
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
why a young man doesn't go all the way.

317
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
It's important that we create

318
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
the environment where every boy

319
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
who has the passion and the drive

320
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
and the interest and with a little nudging-

321
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
and that's fine, nudging is good-

322
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
can become an Eagle Scout.

323
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
but we shouldn't be so focused on producing

324
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Eagle Scouts that we forget

325
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
the impact that we make on every kid

326
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
who gets involved with us over a period of time.

327
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
Scout oath and law you carry with you

328
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
for the rest of your life, regardless of the rank

329
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
you make because you learn it early

330
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
and the more that you practice it

331
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
by being an active member of a Scout troop,

332
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
the more it becomes part of your DNA.

333
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
But it's really important

334
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
that we don't just solely focus

335
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
on producing Eagle Scouts at the expense

336
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
of all those kids who aren't going to get there.

337
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
I think that's really important.

338
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
You ask about people who work with Scouts.

339
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
what can we do to really make a difference

340
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
and, especially today,

341
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
become really good at it,

342
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
learn how to relate to today's kids,

343
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
understand the reality

344
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
that kids face today versus those

345
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
that you bring with you from your own past experience,

346
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and become really good at it?

347
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
It's wonderful that you want to,

348
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
but it's even more wonderful

349
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
that you get good at it by learning

350
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
and those kinds of things.

351
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
I would be remiss

352
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
if I didn't share that.

353
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
How do we get good at it, Bob?

354
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
You learn from the best.

355
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
You learn from people around you

356
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
Because you have done it for so long.

357
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
there are lots of things that have changed.

358
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
You have to embrace change.

359
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
For example, I love

360
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
the discussion that I have

361
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
with old-time Scout masters

362
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
who are really, really good guys

363
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
but are angry at me for allowing

364
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
them to take off on the left shirt.

365
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
left sleeve of the Boy Scout shirt.

366
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
I love having the conversation with them

367
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
about. tell me what the difference is

368
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
between a young man reaching into his backpack

369
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
and pulling out a field book,

370
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
coming through the pages to see if he can eat that plant

371
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
or if that snake is going to eat him,

372
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
or reaching into his pocket

373
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
and pulling out the very same device

374
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
that he uses to get every other bit of information

375
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
in his life to accomplish the same thing.

376
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
It's just a tool.

377
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
Embracing things like technology

378
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
that are creative in our approach

379
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
and embracing the notion that

380
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
kids are different today

381
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
and if we want to impact them

382
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
the way we know we can,

383
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
we have to meet them where they are,

384
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
at least meet them halfway.

385
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
Those are things that especially people

386
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
have been around for a while

387
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
need to understand and embrace.

388
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
You will have been our chief scout executive

389
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
for five years in September,

390
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
Correct?

391
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
And now you were telling me

392
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
we have a mandatory retirement

393
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
for the CEO, only

394
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
for the chief scout executive

395
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
of 65, and it's a good rule.

396
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
It really is.

397
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
When I started this adventure,

398
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
I knew I would only have five years to leave the footprint

399
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
that my predecessors

400
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
have had 8,, 10,, 20 years to leave.

401
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
So I set myself on a journey

402
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
of sprinting

403
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
and I've been doing that

404
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
and I'm proud of what I've accomplished.

405
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
I'm proud of where scouting is today

406
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
and what we've done.

407
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
But if you move that finish line I think I'd die.

408
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
I'm not quite sure how I'd get there.

409
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
So it's a good rule

410
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
and I celebrate that, I embrace that

411
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
and there's a lot of great folks

412
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
that the selection committee is looking at

413
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
that will succeed me

414
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
and take us to New Heights.

415
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
But it's been a great adventure

416
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
Over your tenure. what do you think

417
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
is the most exciting and promising thing

418
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
that's happening in the Boy Scouts of America right now?

419
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
There's a couple.

420
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
It's such a major exercise

421
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
of reinventing our culture.

422
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
You can change the organization chart all you want,

423
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
but changing the hearts and minds of the people in the boxes,

424
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
that's a whole other story.

425
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
We have really sort of redefined

426
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
what we call kind of fund, the new BFA,

427
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
in ways that help us

428
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
learn how to serve our customer

429
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
better, whoever the customer happens to be.

430
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
and in the case of the national organization,

431
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
I maintain that there's only two reasons

432
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
for the national council to exist,

433
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
and one is to preserve and protect

434
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
the brand, because as that brand becomes

435
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
more iconic: all the boats rise.

436
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
and the other is to help a local council

437
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
be successful because all scouting

438
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
is local.

439
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
The 1325 Walnut Hill Lane-

440
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
the national headquarters of the Boy Scouts- owns one unit.

441
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
it's a venture crew for employees.

442
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
that's it. Scouting happens

443
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
in Peoria

444
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
and Kenneth Square and places like that,

445
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
so kind of reinventing that.

446
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
and then this whole notion

447
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
of learning how to embrace the world

448
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
we live in today with technology

449
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
and the STEM initiative

450
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
and the healthy living initiative,

451
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
which are addressing national crises

452
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
that we have today with regard to

453
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
science and technology and with regard to children's health.

454
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
those are things that I'm really proud of.

455
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
and then the iconic sort of

456
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
cherry on the Sunday is

457
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
the summit project in West Virginia,

458
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
which is sort of the boldest thing we could do

459
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
to proclaim that we're going to be around

460
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
for another hundred years. Very intentional

461
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
about talking about the future rather than the past.

462
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
And what do you think that second century

463
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
is going to look like?

464
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
Well, I can ask a lot of you:

465
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
what are you going to look like a hundred years from now?

466
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
Frankly, I don't have a clue. when you think about it,

467
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
We may be teleporting around.

468
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
we don't even know what transportation is,

469
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
but I will tell you this- and I say this

470
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
to anybody who cares to listen-

471
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
that as long as trustworthy, loyal, helpful,

472
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
friendly, courteous, kind, obedient, cheerful,

473
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
thrifty, brave, clean and reverent

474
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
and concepts like on my honor, I'll do my duty,

475
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
honor and duty and God

476
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
and country- as long as those concepts are relevant,

477
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
then scouting will be relevant

478
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
and be strong a hundred years from now.

479
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
but if those concepts

480
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
are no longer relevant, we're all in trouble

481
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
for bigger reasons than just scouting.

482
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
I really believe that as long as we continue

483
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
to adapt to the world around us

484
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
in ways of delivering to the kids

485
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
around us, at whatever point in time we are,

486
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
we will continue to be successful

487
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
as long as we maintain our roots, our core values.

488
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
The contribution we've made

489
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
to America for a hundred years

490
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
is producing individual good citizens

491
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
and we will always need

492
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
individual good citizens.

493
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
so I can't even dream

494
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
at the rate of technology

495
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
and other things going today.

496
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
I can't even dream what we're going to look like ten years from now.

497
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
but the point is

498
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
that those bedrock values that we've stood

499
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
forever for

500
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
and have stood the test of time

501
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
had better always be important,

502
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
and as long as they're important

503
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
we'll continue to deliver.

504
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
Thank you very much. If I can ever be of any service to you, sir,

505
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
the podcast can let us know

506
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,000
You already are. Thank you, Appreciate it.

507
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
Appreciate it

508
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
A symbol of power, nobility and freedom.

509
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
the eagle's flight through human history

